{
	title: 'Hal.app: macOS desktop window for the web client',
	spec: "mac/Hal.swift is a small Swift app (one window, WKWebView, an Edit menu so Cmd-C/V/A/Z work) that shows Hal's existing web client and its tabs; no new project UI. The app holds no Hal code or Bun: built from a checkout, it records that checkout and the bun path in its Info.plist and runs that checkout's src/main.ts with this home (~/.hal, or --home <dir> given via open --args). On launch it starts hal serve as a child; a hosting terminal hands over (fd1); if another hal serve hosts, the child exits 1 and the app joins that host and leaves it running. It restarts its child on exit 100 (/restart host) and on quit SIGTERMs only its own child. It learns the web address and a login by running hal auth, which asks the running host over the owner-only host socket, and opening its Open: <url>/?auth=<one-time code> line; the url comes from the host's auth-code event (task crv): the webUrl setting, else localhost at the port the web server really listens on (webPort or the next free port through 9100, never assumed). No port, code or token is written to state/ or anywhere else; the cookie lives in WebKit's store like a browser's. A ?auth= link opened by an already logged-in browser keeps its cookie and mints no new token, so launches do not grow state/web-sessions.ason. Until the host answers (up to 30 s) the window says Hal is starting; a failure shows hal serve's and hal auth's output in the window. While a running hal finishes a long-running job before handing over, the window says so after 0.5 s and waits without the 30 s deadline. New-window links to other sites open in the default browser; Hal's own pages open in another app window that shares the login. ./install on macOS asks 'Install the Mac app (Hal.app in ~/Applications)? Terminal and web work without it. [y/N]' when the app is missing and stdin is a terminal; default no, also with -y. Without swiftc it says to run xcode-select --install and goes on without the app. An installed app is rebuilt when mac/ changes (a hash stored in the bundle). Later, not now: a DMG that clones the official repo to ~/.hal and runs ./install, an Install hal command menu item, hal serve --standby.",
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Prototype, measured 2026-11-13 on an M-series Mac (macOS 27.2, scratch home): launch to live WebSocket 0.46-0.76 s; footprint of all app processes 175 MB (Hal 21, hal serve 56, WebContent 79, GPU 14, Networking 6); idle CPU over 60 s 0.25% total, mostly hal serve (0.15 s), the same with the window hidden. Reading the login shell environment (zsh -ilc env) costs about 70 ms. Five launches left one token in web-sessions.ason.',
		'A GUI app gets launchd PATH only, so tools would miss bun, Homebrew and ~/.local/bin: the app reads $SHELL -ilc env once (5 s cap) and gives it to its children. The app waits for its own serve to log web on before asking hal auth, else a terminal handing over could answer with an address about to vanish. Not handled yet: a crashed app orphans its hal serve (the next launch joins it); no app icon; notifications in Hal.app are untested.',
		'A non-empty ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL in the app environment (benchmarks set it with launchctl setenv) becomes hal serve --anthropic-url.'
	],
	needs: ['fd1', 'kp0', 'hd', '8a', 'crv']
}
